The '''2019 UEFA Super Cup''' was the 44th edition of the [[UEFA Super Cup]], an annual [[association football|football]] match organised by [[UEFA]] and contested by the reigning champions of the two main European club competitions, the [[UEFA Champions League]] and the [[UEFA Europa League]]. The match featured two [[The Football Association|English]] sides, [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], the winners of the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League]], and [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], the winners of the [[2018–19 UEFA Europa League]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0252-0caca214aeb5-a487c182c43c-1000--2019-uefa-super-cup-liverpool-v-chelsea/ |title=2019 UEFA Super Cup: Liverpool v Chelsea |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 June 2019}}</ref> The match was played at [[Vodafone Park]] in [[Istanbul]], [[Turkey]] on 14 August 2019.<ref name="venue">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/newsid=2502786.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170920181024/http://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/newsid=2502786.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 September 2017 |title=Madrid to host UEFA Champions League Final 2019 |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=20 September 2017 |access-date=20 September 2017}}</ref> The match was the first all-English UEFA Super Cup, and the eighth overall Super Cup to feature two teams from the same country. For the first time, the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR) system was used in the competition.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2573203.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20181014043150/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2573203.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 October 2018 |title=VAR to be introduced in 2019/20 UEFA Champions League |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 September 2018 |access-date=27 September 2018}}</ref>

Liverpool won the match 5–4 on penalties following a 2–2 draw after extra time for their fourth UEFA Super Cup title.<ref name="motm"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Adrian the hero as Liverpool beat Chelsea on penalties to win Super Cup |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49332825 |date=14 August 2019 |access-date=14 August 2019 |agency=BBC}}</ref> As winners, Liverpool were rewarded £4 million in [[prize money]].<ref name="Liverpool at Club World Cup: Five reasons why Qatar tournament matters to Reds">{{citation|title = Liverpool at Club World Cup: Five reasons why Qatar tournament matters to Reds|url = https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/50763243|publisher = BBC Sport|date = 18 December 2019|access-date = 19 December 2019}}</ref>

