==Background==
The match was the first UEFA Super Cup to feature two English teams, after all-English finals in both of UEFA's seasonal tournaments; [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] beat [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] in the [[2019 UEFA Champions League Final|Champions League]] and [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] beat [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] in the [[2019 UEFA Europa League Final|Europa League]]. The match was the eighth overall Super Cup to feature two teams from the same country, previously achieved five times by Spanish teams ([[2006 UEFA Super Cup|2006]], [[2014 UEFA Super Cup|2014]], [[2015 UEFA Super Cup|2015]], [[2016 UEFA Super Cup|2016]] and [[2018 UEFA Super Cup|2018]]) and twice by Italian teams ([[1990 European Super Cup|1990]] and [[1993 European Super Cup|1993]]). This also ensured that it would be the first Super Cup to be won by an English team since Liverpool in [[2005 UEFA Super Cup|2005]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/sup.html |title=European Super Cup |website=RSSSF.com |publisher=[[Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation]] |date=16 August 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019}}</ref>

This was the eleventh meeting between both clubs in UEFA competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefasupercup/2019/e/e_2025638_pk.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190920044730/https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefasupercup/2019/e/e_2025638_pk.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 September 2019 |title=Match Press Kit |date=14 August 2019 |work=[[UEFA]] |access-date=15 August 2019}}</ref> Liverpool and Chelsea were drawn against each other in five successive Champions League seasons between [[2004–05 UEFA Champions League|2004–05]] and [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League|2008–09]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0254-0d3ee047a232-06c7e8e7e350-1000--super-cup-preview-liverpool-v-chelsea/ |title=Super Cup preview: Liverpool v Chelsea |date=13 August 2019 |work=[[UEFA]] |access-date=14 August 2019}}</ref> Chelsea had won three of these meetings (including one [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|after extra time]]) to Liverpool's two, with the other five ending in draws (including one decided on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]] in Liverpool's favour). Each side had advanced against the other two times apiece, with the other tie coming in the [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League]] [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group G|group stage]] as Liverpool's unique qualifying situation that season did not grant them association protection.<ref name="supercupfacts">{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0254-0d329cf5d9bc-eecaec27abfd-1000--super-cup-facts-liverpool-v-chelsea/ |title=Super Cup facts: Liverpool v Chelsea |date=12 August 2019 |work=[[UEFA]] |access-date=15 August 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool get in Champions League |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4613695.stm |date=10 June 2005 |access-date=15 August 2019 |agency=BBC}}</ref>

The teams have met twice previously in domestic cup finals, which Chelsea have triumphed on both occasions. First was in the [[2005 Football League Cup Final]], played outside England at the [[Millennium Stadium]] in [[Wales]] while [[Wembley Stadium]] was being rebuilt, where they won 3–2 after extra time, and more recently in the [[2012 FA Cup Final]] at Wembley, winning 2–1.<ref name="supercupfacts" />  They both also contested the English super cup, the [[FA Community Shield]], in [[2006 FA Community Shield|2006]] and Liverpool won 2–1 on that occasion at [[Cardiff]]'s Millennium Stadium.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/4780739.stm |title=Chelsea 1–2 Liverpool |agency=BBC |date=13 August 2006 |access-date=22 December 2017}}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:2017293155419 2017-10-20 Fussball Frauen Deutschland vs Island - Sven - 1D X MK II - 0017 - B70I0638.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Stéphanie Frappart]], the [[referee]] for the match.]]

On 2 August 2019, UEFA named French official [[Stéphanie Frappart]] as the referee for the match, marking the first time in history a woman would referee the final of a UEFA men's competition. Frappart has been a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] since 2009,<ref>{{cite web |title=Referees: France |url=https://www.fifa.com/development/education-and-technical/referees/association=fra/index.html |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=5 July 2019 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190707083706/https://www.fifa.com/development/education-and-technical/referees/association=fra/index.html |archive-date=7 July 2019}}</ref> and had officiated at the [[2019 FIFA Women's World Cup]] in the month prior, where she was appointed as the referee for the [[2019 FIFA Women's World Cup Final|final]].<ref>{{cite news |date=6 July 2019 |title=Frappart: Final role a huge source of pride |url=https://www.fifa.com/womensworldcup/news/frappart-final-role-a-huge-sense-of-pride |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=6 July 2019 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190706143910/https://www.fifa.com/womensworldcup/news/frappart-final-role-a-huge-sense-of-pride |archive-date=6 July 2019}}</ref> She also previously officiated at the [[2015 FIFA Women's World Cup]], [[Football at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Women's tournament|2016 Summer Olympics]] and [[UEFA Women's Euro 2017]],<ref>{{cite news |date=12 March 2018 |title=Stéphanie Frappart retenue parmi les arbitres |language=fr |trans-title=Stéphanie Frappart among selected referees |url=https://www.eurosport.fr/football/coupe-du-monde-feminine/2019/stephanie-frappart-retenue-parmi-les-arbitres_sto7037659/story.shtml |publisher=[[Eurosport]] |agency=Agence France-Presse |access-date=6 July 2019}}</ref> and became the first woman to referee in [[Ligue 1]] in April 2019.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/french-ligue-1/story/3837476/stephanie-frappart-becomes-first-woman-to-referee-ligue-1-match |title=Ligue of her own: Female ref makes historic debut |website=ESPN |date=28 April 2019 |access-date=5 July 2019}}</ref> Her compatriot Manuela Nicolosi was chosen as one of the assistant referees, along with Irish official Michelle O'Neill, while [[Cüneyt Çakır]] of Turkey was chosen as the fourth official. French referee [[Clément Turpin]] was named the [[video assistant referee]], presiding over the first use of the technology in the UEFA Super Cup. His fellow countryman [[François Letexier]] was named as one of the assistant video assistant referees for the match, along with [[Massimiliano Irrati]] of Italy, while Mark Borsch of Germany offside VAR.<ref name="officials">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news/newsid=2617551.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191110060858/https://www.uefa.com/news/newsid=2617551.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 November 2019 |title=Stéphanie Frappart to referee UEFA Super Cup |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 August 2019 |access-date=2 August 2019}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Liverpool vs. Chelsea, UEFA Super Cup 2019-08-14 53.jpg|thumb|left|[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] winger [[Sadio Mané]] lifting the [[UEFA Super Cup]] after scoring twice against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in the final.]]

Chelsea took the lead after 36 minutes when a pass from [[Christian Pulisic]] played in [[Olivier Giroud]] on the left and his first-time left-foot shot found the right corner of the net. Pulisic had a second goal ruled out shortly after for offside after confirmation from VAR. [[Sadio Mané]] made it 1–1 after 48 minutes when he scored from close range at the second attempt after a flick past the goalkeeper from substitute [[Roberto Firmino]]. The match went to extra time and Sadio Mané got his second goal of the match in the 95th minute with a side-foot into the top right-hand corner of the net after Roberto Firmino found him with a cut-back from the left.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/liverpool-vs-chelsea-super-cup-jurgen-klopp-adrian-penalty-shootout-win-goals-a9059081.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190815075249/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/liverpool-vs-chelsea-super-cup-jurgen-klopp-adrian-penalty-shootout-win-goals-a9059081.html |archive-date=2019-08-15 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |title=Super Cup joy for Jurgen Klopp as Adrian clinches penalty shootout glory |date=14 August 2019 |work=The Independent |access-date=15 August 2019}}</ref>

Chelsea were awarded a penalty six minutes later when [[Adrián (footballer)|Adrián]] was adjudged to have brought down [[Tammy Abraham]] when he ran onto a pass in the penalty area. [[Jorginho (footballer, born December 1991)|Jorginho]] scored to make it 2–2 with a low shot to the right corner. The match went to a penalty shoot-out and with the score at 5–4 Tammy Abraham saw his low shot saved by Adrián with his right leg to win the game for Liverpool.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/aug/14/liverpool-chelsea-uefa-super-cup-match-report |title=Liverpool sink Chelsea to lift Super Cup thanks to Adrián shootout save |date=14 August 2019 |work=Guardian |access-date=15 August 2019}}</ref>

